  "notes": "COMMON PATTERNS:\n{強|し}いる is an ichidan verb despite ending in いる. The passive form {強|し}いられる is extremely common and often more natural than the active form.\n- AにBを{強|し}いる (to force B on A)\n- {強|し}いられる (to be forced/compelled — very common passive)\n- {強|し}いて{言|い}えば (if forced to say; if I had to say — set phrase)\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {犠牲|ぎせい}を{強|し}いる (to demand sacrifice)\n- {我慢|がまん}を{強|し}いる (to force someone to endure)\n- {負担|ふたん}を{強|し}いる (to impose a burden)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n{強制|きょうせい}する is a suru-verb meaning to compel or enforce, often used for rules or systems. {強|し}いる is used more for interpersonal situations or abstract impositions.",
